    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d519">

                <pb facs="0629" n="623" xml:id="pg623"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. W. Averell
                        Harriman, Special Representative of the President, to President
                            Roosevelt
                    </hi><anchor n="24" xml:id="d519-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-08-15T10:10:00+03:00">August 15,
                            1942—10:10 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 2:14 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>We dined in force at Kremlin last night. All members of Soviet Defence Committee
                    as well as a number of members of General Staff were present.</p>
                <p>Stalin was in best of spirits and was most cordial to Prime Minister and myself.
                    He seemed to be entirely oblivious of the unpleasant discussions of night
                    before. Prime Minister, however, arrived still somewhat annoyed from rough
                    treatment he had received but as evening progressed he became more and more
                    interested in his talks with Stalin which ranged from theories of military
                    tactics to post-war policies. He spoke in some detail of sending an air squadron
                    to Southern Front and indicated that it was a subject you had in mind too, if it
                    were feasible.</p>
                <p>I expect you will hear from Prime Minister on this subject.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d519-f1" n="24" type="source" xml:id="d519-f1-content">
                <p>Copy obtained from the Franklin D. Roosevelt Library, Hyde Park, N. Y. This
                    telegram was sent through the Naval Attaché in Moscow.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
